WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday, I'm in the office

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.560
So I don't know if you've seen Sarah's brilliant art assignment video, The Case for Kanye West,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.220
but after watching it, I've been listening more carefully to

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.320
Kanye's music, and there's a lyric in the song "Power" that's really

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.500
stuck with me. It goes, "They say I was the abomination of Obama's nation. Well, that's

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:20.960
a pretty bad way to start a conversation." I think the grammatical ambiguity of "that's a pretty bad way to start a conversation"

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.800
is important here. Sidenote! Don't tell me Kanye didn't mean any of this because he's not that smart or whatever

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.500
One, authorial intent doesn't matter, and two: Kanye is that smart. I mean,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.900
sometimes he's an offensive buffoon, but so are a lot of smart people.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.500
Right, so, saying "You're the abomination of Obama's nation" is indeed

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.660
a pretty bad way to start a conversation, right? Because you're calling someone a name, you're also

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.340
using cleverness to make yourself seem correct.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.100
But "They say I was the abomination of Obama's nation" is also

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.560
a pretty bad way to start a conversation, I mean, first off, you're using a non-specific "they"

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.600
as a way of constructing a false us-versus-them dichotomy

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.100
Also, most Kanye haters weren't actually calling him "the abomination of Obama's nation"

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.780
they were criticizing other things about him and his work, but then Kanye simplified

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.540
and repackaged those arguments in a way that made them seem ridiculous.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.800
You see these bad ways of starting conversations a lot in contemporary discourse.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.080
Think of all the "got you" moments in the political 24-hour news cycle

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.420
and the use of diminutives and name-calling.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.900
I mean, from Canadian Cruz to Crooked Hillary, to Lying Donald to Barry Obama, they all

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.740
they all undermine and demean people in a way that's, like,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.540
a little too vague to defend yourself against.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.460
There's the rhetorical questions, the certainty of one's own opinion,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.780
wit masquerading as accuracy, the sharing of one study

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.800
you read about once instead of doing the research to learn

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.200
what the bulk of evidence currently points to....

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.280
The presumption that those who disagree with you are merely wrong--

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.560
the list goes on and on. My favorite at the moment is where you accuse

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.420
a voice you don't like of being shrill or shouty

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.360
or aggressive, even when it isn't, which is a tactic

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.500
so meta, even Kanye couldn't parse it.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.620
I do this too, by the way. Like the other day I said on twitter

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.380
that forty percent of U.S. gun sales

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.440
involve no background checks, which turns out to be

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.720
a totally unsupported statistic

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.720
but by the time I did the relevant research

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.540
the tweet was already out there, furthering the ceaseless

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.720
spread of misinformation.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.900
So I don't get a pass on this one.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.900
But research shows that rude, aggressive,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.620
and inaccurate comments don't just lower

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.900
the overall quality of conversation, they also

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.000
make people feel more angry and afraid.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.460
And while I think Kanye is right about

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.380
all these bad ways of starting conversations,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.900
they're effective ways to start conversations

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.500
because we all feel compelled to respond.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.720
Like, hostility generally gets answered with hostility.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.720
And when the outrage button gets pressed inside of us

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.520
we want to share and reply

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.680
and do all the other things that the

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.100
architecture of social media currently values.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.900
So trolling is poisonous, but it works.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.300
And that's why the front page of most YouTube comment sections

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.040
doesn't contain the most interesting, or generous, or nuanced comments,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.280
but instead the most controversial ones.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.360
And I know those controversial comments get replies

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.100
but I don't think they change anyone's mind.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.040
Like, screaming at each other is a bad way of listening,

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.520
but I think it's also a bad way of getting heard.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.160
Now I realize that's easy for me to say since I already have a platform

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.440
but I truly don't think anyone has ever changed their mind

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.120
because they were called an idiot.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.860
Hank, I really loved your video last week about race and racism

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.020
in the United States, but the comments section

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.400
was a disaster.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.860
Until, you made a 'hankschannel' video asking people

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.940
to simply reply with a plus sign to comments they agreed with.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.300
That immediately tricked the YouTube comment algorithm

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.660
into valuing generous and thoughtful comments

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:20.780
which, in turn, made commenting far more civil and productive.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.720
It's a small thing and it wasn't perfect but it made

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.100
a real change in that online conversation,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.840
and I know I'll be using the plus sign reply from here on out.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.840
Nerdfighters, if you have other suggestions for how we

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.340
might improve the quality of conversations both here

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.720
and in the wider world, I'll be in the comments

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.500
doing my best to listen.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.720
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

